\begin{abstract}
Multiplication is traditionally defined as repeated addition or scaling, yet these interpretations fail to account for cases where multiplication results in preservation (\( \times 1 \)), nullification (\( \times 0 \)), or sign reversal (\( \times -1 \)). This paper introduces a \textit{Generalized Multiplicative Transformation (GMT) Framework}, in which multiplication is decomposed into four distinct operations: \textbf{Expansion, Reduction, Reversal, and Nullification}. We rigorously demonstrate that these operations obey the commutative, associative, and distributive laws and clarify the conceptual distinctions between scaling and reversal. Applications in mathematics education, computational algorithms, quantum computing, and neural network design are discussed. This framework not only resolves inconsistencies in traditional multiplication but also paves the way for novel approaches in applied mathematics and computer science.
\end{abstract}

