 \section{Introduction}
 \subsection{Background and Motivation}
Multiplication is often introduced as repeated addition (for whole numbers) or as scaling (for real numbers). However, this oversimplified view fails to address the full range of multiplicative behavior. Consider:
 \begin{itemize}
     \item \(5 \times 1 = 5\) (the identity case, where no scaling occurs),
     \item \(5 \times 0 = 0\) (a nullifying operation),
     \item \(5 \times -1 = -5\) (a sign-reversal operation).
 \end{itemize}
These examples indicate that conventional multiplication conflates several distinct operations. This paper proposes a refined framework that decomposes multiplication into four transformation types—each with its own axiomatic basis.

 \subsection{Research Questions}
This paper addresses the following:
 \begin{enumerate}
    \item How can multiplication be more accurately defined to account for expansion, reduction, reversal, and nullification?
    \item What algebraic properties (commutativity, associativity, distributivity) are exhibited by these operations?
    \item What are the implications of adopting the GMT framework for mathematics education, computational methods, quantum computing, and neural network design?
 \end{enumerate}

