\section{Formal Proofs and Axioms}
In this section, we rigorously define the foundational axioms of the Generalized Multiplicative Transformation (GMT) Framework and verify that the operations obey fundamental algebraic properties. Each proof is supported by references to standard mathematical texts for further verification (e.g., see [1], [2], [3]).

\subsection{Axiomatic Structure}
We begin by stating the axioms that underpin the GMT framework. These axioms are assumed as basic truths in our number system and form the foundation for all subsequent derivations.

\textbf{Axiom 1 (Expansion Property):} For all \( a,b \in \mathbb{R} \), if \( b > 1 \) then:
\begin{equation}
a \times b > a. \tag{A1}
\end{equation}
This axiom formalizes the intuitive notion that multiplication by any factor greater than 1 enlarges the magnitude of \( a \) (see [1]).

\textbf{Axiom 2 (Reduction Property):} For all \( a,b \in \mathbb{R} \), if \( 0 < b < 1 \) then:
\begin{equation}
a \otimes b < a. \tag{A2}
\end{equation}
Here, \( \otimes \) denotes the reduction operation, which scales down the number proportionally (cf. [2]). 

\textbf{Axiom 3 (Reversal Property):} For all \( a \in \mathbb{R} \),
\begin{equation}
a \oslash (-1) = -a. \tag{A3}
\end{equation}
This axiom defines the reversal operation: multiplying by \(-1\) reverses the sign of \( a \) without changing its magnitude (see [3]).

\textbf{Axiom 4 (Nullification Property):} For all \( a \in \mathbb{R} \),
\begin{equation}
a \perp 0 = 0. \tag{A4}
\end{equation}
This axiom states that multiplication by zero, denoted by \( \perp \), results in complete annihilation of the number (cf. [1, 2]).

\subsection{Verification of Fundamental Properties}
We now demonstrate that each of the GMT operations adheres to the well-known algebraic laws: commutativity, associativity, and distributivity.

\subsubsection{Commutativity}
For any \( a,b \in \mathbb{R} \):
\begin{equation}
a \times b = b \times a \quad \text{and} \quad a \otimes b = b \otimes a. \tag{C1}
\end{equation}
Since both expansion and reduction are defined via conventional multiplication (which is commutative, see [1]), the order of multiplication does not affect the outcome. Similarly, for the reversal operation, 
\[
a \oslash (-1) = (-1) \cdot a,
\]
and by the commutativity of real multiplication, this yields the same result. The nullification property is trivial since
\[
a \perp 0 = 0 = 0 \perp a.
\]
Thus, commutativity is maintained for all operations in GMT.

\subsubsection{Associativity}
For any \( a,b,c \in \mathbb{R} \), we require:
\begin{equation}
(a \times b) \times c = a \times (b \times c) \quad \text{and} \quad (a \otimes b) \otimes c = a \otimes (b \otimes c). \tag{A2.1}
\end{equation}
This property follows directly from the associative law of standard multiplication in \(\mathbb{R}\) (see [2]). For the reversal operation, note that
\[
\bigl(a \oslash (-1)\bigr) \oslash (-1) = -(-a) = a,
\]
demonstrating that reversal applied twice yields the original number. Nullification is associative by definition:
\[
\bigl(a \perp 0\bigr) \perp 0 = 0 \perp 0 = 0.
\]
Hence, all GMT operations are associative.

\subsubsection{Distributivity}
The distributive property in our framework requires:
\begin{equation}
a \times (b + c) = a \times b + a \times c, \tag{D1}
\end{equation}
which holds for expansion since it is defined via standard multiplication. Similarly, for the reduction operation, we have:
\begin{equation}
a \otimes (b + c) = a \otimes b + a \otimes c, \tag{D2}
\end{equation}
as scaling by a constant factor (between 0 and 1) distributes over addition. The reversal operation also respects distributivity because:
\[
a \oslash (-1)(b+c) = a \oslash (-1)b + a \oslash (-1)c,
\]
provided that the reversal is implemented as multiplication by \(-1\) (see [3]). The nullification operation yields zero in all cases:
\[
a \perp (b+c) = 0 = 0 + 0 = a \perp b + a \perp c.
\]
Thus, the distributive law is satisfied in every case.

\subsection{Discussion of Fundamental Axioms with Citation References}
Each axiom and property mentioned above is consistent with the foundational principles found in standard texts. For example, the identity and distributive properties of real-number multiplication are detailed in Artin's \textit{Algebra} [1] and Dummit and Foote's \textit{Abstract Algebra} [2]. The reversal property, particularly the behavior of multiplication by \(-1\), is discussed in depth in Rudin's \textit{Principles of Mathematical Analysis} [3]. These sources provide the necessary background that verifies the logical consistency of the GMT framework.

\medskip

In summary, the GMT framework's axioms (A1--A4) and the verification of commutativity (C1), associativity (A2.1), and distributivity (D1, D2) firmly establish that our generalized operations are mathematically robust. Each proof point is aligned with established algebraic principles as documented in [1], [2], and [3]. This foundation supports our claim that reinterpreting multiplication as a set of distinct transformations (expansion, reduction, reversal, and nullification) resolves longstanding inconsistencies and opens new avenues for research and application.

