\section{Conclusion and Future Research}
\subsection{Summary of Findings}
The Generalized Multiplicative Transformation (GMT) Framework redefines multiplication as a composite of four operations—expansion, reduction, reversal, and nullification—rather than a single homogeneous process. This approach resolves ambiguities in traditional definitions, enhances the clarity of mathematical education, and provides promising applications in computing, quantum physics, and AI.

\subsection{Open Questions and Future Directions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Can the GMT framework be extended to encompass division and other arithmetic operations?
    \item What new algebraic structures or number systems emerge when these transformation types are taken as fundamental?
    \item How can GMT be concretely integrated into computational hardware and neural network architectures to reduce error propagation?
    \item What experimental validations in quantum computing can support the theoretical advantages of GMT?
\end{itemize}

